🌐 Network Devices



📌 Introduction

Network Devices (Networking Hardware) are physical appliances required for communication and interaction between computers in a computer network.

They act as the “plumbing of the internet”, responsible for:

Directing data traffic

Amplifying signals

Securing network connections

To understand their operation clearly, network devices are categorized based on the OSI Model layers they operate on.

🔹 Functions of Network Devices

Enable communication by transmitting and receiving data

Allow devices to connect to networks securely and efficiently

Improve network performance by managing traffic and reducing congestion

Provide security by controlling access and blocking unauthorized activity

Extend network coverage and overcome signal loss (attenuation)

🧩 Types of Network Devices (Based on OSI Model)
🔸 Layer 1 Devices – Physical Layer

These devices work with raw bits (electrical/optical signals).
They do not understand MAC or IP addresses.

1️⃣ Hub

Function:

Central connection point for devices in a LAN

How it Works:

Receives data on one port and broadcasts it to all ports

Drawbacks:

High collisions

Security risk (all devices receive all data)

Status:

❌ Obsolete

✅ Replaced by Switches

2️⃣ Repeater

Function:

Regenerates weak signals

How it Works:

Receives weak signal → amplifies → retransmits

Use Case:

Extending Wi-Fi range

Ethernet beyond 100 meters

3️⃣ Modem (Modulator–Demodulator)

Function:

Connects home/office network to ISP

How it Works:

Digital → Analog (Modulation)

Analog → Digital (Demodulation)

🔸 Layer 2 Devices – Data Link Layer

These devices work with MAC Addresses and are smarter than hubs.

4️⃣ Switch

Function:

Connects devices within a LAN

How it Works:

Learns MAC addresses

Sends data only to the destination port

Benefits:

Reduced collisions

Improved speed and security

Types:

Unmanaged: Plug-and-play (Home/Small office)

Managed: VLANs, QoS (Enterprise networks)

5️⃣ Bridge

Function:

Connects two LAN segments

How it Works:

Filters traffic using MAC addresses

Reduces unnecessary traffic

Note:

Mostly replaced by Switches

6️⃣ Access Point (AP)

Function:

Creates a Wireless LAN (WLAN)

How it Works:

Bridges wired Ethernet and wireless devices

Difference from Router:

AP provides Wi-Fi only

Router assigns IP addresses and routes traffic

🔸 Layer 3 Devices – Network Layer

These devices work with IP Addresses and connect networks.

7️⃣ Router

Function:

Connects multiple networks (LAN ↔ WAN)

How it Works:

Uses IP addresses

Maintains a Routing Table to select best path

Key Feature:

Stops broadcast traffic

Isolates networks

8️⃣ Brouter (Bridge + Router)

Function:

Hybrid of Bridge and Router

How it Works:

Routes known protocols (TCP/IP)

Bridges unknown or legacy protocols

Status:

Rarely used today

🔸 Layer 4–7 Devices – Advanced Processing
9️⃣ Gateway

Function:

Translates between dissimilar networks

How it Works:

Converts protocols, data formats, or architectures

Example:

Internet Gateway connecting private LAN to public internet

🔟 Firewall

Function:

Network security guard

Role:

Monitors and controls incoming & outgoing traffic

Types:

Hardware Firewall: Dedicated appliance

Software Firewall: Installed on systems

Techniques:

Packet Filtering

Stateful Inspection

📊 Summary: Hub vs Switch vs Router
Feature	Hub	Switch	Router
OSI Layer	Layer 1	Layer 2	Layer 3
Address Type	None (Bits)	MAC Address	IP Address
Data Flow	Broadcast	Unicast	Best Path Routing
Intelligence	Dumb	Smart	Smarter
Usage	Obsolete	LAN Devices	Network Connection
